 continue to go into rwanda and write a  check or  donate a camera or you show them how to  use a camera but um then they leave  and  we  you know we really wanted to create  something that would outlast our film  and help them find work so we partnered  with bps  and the academy of motion pictures to  create a website called rwandafilm.org  we have stickers outside and i urge all  of you to check it out it's really  incredible and bps has done such an  amazing job leading the charge on this  website and  it's really like a linkedin for for east  african filmmakers right now it's mainly  rwandan filmmakers but i i have a  feeling it's gonna it's gonna spread but  we don't wanna run it from across the  world we want it to be run locally and  so we are looking for  support to do that so all the you had  several different narrators in the film  and i know you kind of do a summation  about what where they are today but it's  is that  is that where they are today are those  folks still  uh is there is there an update on on the  lives of people july update july update  uh  we screened when we were there in july  we went to the countryside and screened  um  the films and also  there is a new theater in in kigali now  it's state-of-the-art theater uh which  it's pretty interesting to go to a  theater in in kigali where people  don't really know  how to go to a movie i mean it's people  are on their phones the whole time and  they show up a half hour late and leave  a half hour early and it's very funny  but um both richmond and rodrigue walked  into the movie  and sat down behind us and uh for us to  listen to rodriguez sitting behind us  crying through the whole movie he was  extremely emotional about it his  emotional guy anyway but um  uh he had just gotten married the day  before um  uh richmond has uh  uh was at the time we were filming with  him he was an editor at a pop magazine  and that has since  ended and now he is a full-time  filmmaker which was really nice he's  sustaining himself as a filmmaker he  just directed a documentary  it was it for me it was really fast  again getting back to this point it was  really fascinating being  there and working with a group of people  who who don't go to movies every day i  mean i think we take that for granted of  having this ex you know it's in our  lives it's something that we do  and  it's just it's just something it's part  of what we do but when you're  it was fascinating for me to be  surrounded by a group of people who were  appreciative of the fact that we were  there but they really had no sense of of  that we were these accomplished  filmmakers but we were there to share  our ideas and our thoughts  and to  share our approach to making movies with  all of them  so there was no vocabulary there and  there's no  a tradition of going to the movies film  economy as they like to call it now is  is getting quite competitive and there's  uh  something called the african digital  media academy um that is pretty  incredible they're they have  state-of-the-art equipment they are  teaching people how to do  really technical post-production skills  like rotoscoping and  these guys are making films we're also  focusing on story focusing on story and  they're building a little green screen  studio which has never been  done in rwanda and um they're gonna  they're  definitely a group to watch in the next  couple years they're doing some amazing  work  it's quickly become savvy we had we  spent two days  uh teaching workshops with eight women  who  totally  got it who totally they cranked out a  movie for their closing ceremony in in  the two days that we spent with them and  i think that was our emphasis there when  we were there as well i mean finally you  know when we realized that you we  couldn't talk about camera angles or or  our editing techniques we really uh  there was a  we there was a discussion amongst the  academy members about how do you solve  these problems how do you talk about  films and again finally we came back to  the idea that what we had to do  was to to really talk about story toy  storytelling where do you get the  stories from  do you get it from your parents do you  do you get it from folk tales  uh you know so  it really  trying to get people to understand how  important storytelling was  as a foundation for making films
 

